    "q-160454": {
      "dom_id": "step-q160454",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function",
      "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x+2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x+2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
